    <class name="ThreadPool"
           c:symbol-prefix="thread_pool"
           c:type="DexThreadPool"
           version="1.2"
           parent="Object"
           final="1"
           glib:type-name="DexThreadPool"
           glib:get-type="dex_thread_pool_get_type"
           glib:fundamental="1">
      <doc xml:space="preserve"
           filename="src/dex-thread-pool.c"
           line="31">`DexThreadPool` is a thread pool for managing native OS threads similar
to [struct@GLib.ThreadPool].

The threads managed by `DexThreadPool` do not contain a
[class@Dex.Scheduler] which means that you cannot await
futures or schedule [class@Dex.Block] from a worker thread.

Threads are created up-front from [ctor@Dex.ThreadPool.new].

`DexThreadPool` primarily exists for situations where you are
using blocking external libraries and want to avoid calling
[func@Dex.thread_spawn] without any sort of queuing or bounding
on the permitted concurrency.</doc>
      <constructor name="new" c:identifier="dex_thread_pool_new" version="1.2">
        <doc xml:space="preserve"
             filename="src/dex-thread-pool.c"
             line="214">Creates a fixed-size pool of reusable operating system threads.</doc>
